  "result": "Fixed. The quote now prints **27.06**, and all 6 tests pass.\n\n**Root cause:** `to_cents` in `shop/money.py` padded a one-digit decimal with a zero on the wrong side. It used `rjust`, which turned `\"12.5\"` into 12 dollars and `\"05\"` cents, so 1205 cents ($12.05) instead of 1250. Two of those come to 2410 cents, and adding tax gave 26.09.\n\n**Fix:** I changed `frac.rjust(2, \"0\")` to `frac.ljust(2, \"0\")` (`shop/money.py:9`), so `\"5\"` now becomes `\"50\"`. The correct total is 2 \u00d7 $12.50 = $25.00, plus 8.25% tax ($2.0625, rounded to $2.06), which is $27.06.\n\nI also tried a few other inputs and they convert correctly: `12`, `12.50`, `12.05`, `0.1` and `3.`. The existing tests never passed a price with one decimal place, which is why they didn't catch this. A regression test for `to_cents(\"12.5\") == 1250` would be worth adding; I haven't added one.",
